---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { commons } from '../config/commons';

const reports = (await getCollection('reports')).filter((r) => r.data.moderation === 'published');
const annotations = (await getCollection('annotations')).filter((a) => a.data.moderation === 'published');

/*
 * A count of published reports would read as a count of real accounts, and
 * right now every one of them is a constructed illustration published to prove
 * the format renders. Saying "1 published report" while that is true would be
 * the first misleading sentence on a site whose entire proposition is that it
 * is not misleading. So the two are counted separately until real ones exist.
 */
const constructed = reports.filter((r) => r.data.label === 'hypothetical' || r.data.label === 'composite');
const lived = reports.filter((r) => !constructed.includes(r));
---

<BaseLayout
	eyebrow="The Commons"
	title="What actually happened, checked before it publishes"
	lede="The other half of the estate. Where the Record says what the rule is and cites a document for every sentence, this holds what happened to people - attributed, labelled for what kind of evidence it is, and read by a moderator before anyone else reads it."
>
	<section class="band band-tight">
		<div class="shell page-grid">
			<div class="prose">
				<h2>Two properties, and this is the other one</h2>
				<p>
					<a href={commons.record.origin} rel="noopener">{commons.record.name}</a> holds what the
					rule is: every sentence on it cites a published document, and the build refuses to
					publish one that does not. That is a good way to say what a statute requires. It is no
					way at all to say what it is like to have a claim denied on a Tuesday.
				</p>
				<p>
					This is where that goes. Accounts of real situations, and notes from people whose
					professional licence has been checked against a public register. Moderated before
					publication, labelled for what kind of evidence they are, and attributed.
				</p>

				<h2>The one rule that holds on both sides</h2>
				<div class="callout" data-tone="stop">
					<h3>No verdicts</h3>
					<p>{commons.verdictProhibition}</p>
				</div>
				<p>
					That is not squeamishness. A forum full of strangers declaring what should have been
					covered is worth nothing to anybody. A record of accounts saying <em>this happened, here
					is what turned on it, here is who decided</em> is worth a great deal, and the second one
					is only possible if the first is not allowed.
				</p>

				<h2>What makes an account worth citing</h2>
				<p>
					An anonymous thread is not citable — not by a researcher, not by a lawyer, not by us.
					"My claim got denied and it was unfair" carries no date, no state, no line of business,
					no document, and no way to tell whether it happened. Volume of that does not add up to
					authority.
				</p>
				<p>
					So every report here carries six things, and{' '}
					<a href="/standards">Standards</a> explains why each one is required: what kind of
					evidence it is, why it carries that label, where it came from, what happened, who
					decided, and at least three reasons it does not transfer to anyone else's situation.
				</p>

				<h2>Where this is up to</h2>
				{
					lived.length === 0 ? (
						<div class="empty">
							<h2>No real account has been published yet.</h2>
							<p>
								That is the honest state rather than an outage, and it will be true for a while.
								Accounts are not open, moderation happens before publication rather than after, and
								nothing appears here until somebody has read it.
							</p>
							{constructed.length > 0 && (
								<p>
									What is published is {constructed.length} constructed{' '}
									{constructed.length === 1 ? 'illustration' : 'illustrations'}, labelled{' '}
									<code>hypothetical</code> and saying plainly that it did not happen. It exists so
									a contributor can see what a finished report looks like before writing one, and
									so the pages and machine records around it are real rather than mocked.{' '}
									<a href="/reports">Read it</a>.
								</p>
							)}
							<p>
								<a href="/contribute">What contributing will involve</a> is written up already,
								including the things this site will never ask you for.
							</p>
						</div>
					) : (
						<p>
							{lived.length} published {lived.length === 1 ? 'account' : 'accounts'}
							{constructed.length > 0 && `, ${constructed.length} constructed illustrations`}
							{annotations.length > 0 && `, and ${annotations.length} practitioner notes`}.{' '}
							<a href="/reports">Read them</a>.
						</p>
					)
				}
			</div>

			<aside aria-label="About this site">
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Status</h2>
					<dl>
						<div><dt>Real accounts</dt><dd>{lived.length}</dd></div>
						<div><dt>Constructed illustrations</dt><dd>{constructed.length}</dd></div>
						<div><dt>Practitioner notes</dt><dd>{annotations.length}</dd></div>
						<div><dt>Moderation</dt><dd>Before publication</dd></div>
						<div><dt>Accounts</dt><dd>Not open yet</dd></div>
					</dl>
				</div>
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Never collected</h2>
					<ul>
						<li>Any document upload</li>
						<li>A policy or claim number</li>
						<li>A date of birth or government id</li>
						<li>Health information</li>
						<li>A payment method</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
